It is the intent and policy of the County Board of Commissioners:
(1) To promote the health, safety, convenience, order, prosperity, and general welfare of the County and its residents;
(2) To protect the natural resources, the environment, and the public health of Montgomery County;
(3) To facilitate the adequate provision of on-site sewage management for all development in the local jurisdiction;
(4) To protect significant groundwater recharge areas from pollution by spills, discharges, leaks, impoundments, applications of chemicals, injections, and other development;
(5) To promote the wise use of wetlands and protect them from alterations that will significantly affect or reduce their primary functions for water quality, floodplain and erosion control, groundwater recharge, aesthetic natural areas, and wildlife habitat areas;
(6) To establish measures to preserve an adequate supply of safe drinking water and a quality for State waters which is necessary to protect the health and welfare of the public as well as to provide for future growth; to protect the environment, including the soils, air quality, and water resources, from pollution and inappropriate development;
(7) To assist in the orderly, efficient, and integrated development of the County; and to require permits for new development or location within the County.
(Code 2003, ch. 23, art. II, § 2; Res. of 8-7-2001)

423:
In order to promote and provide for uniformity and concert of action among its members, the Commission shall have the following responsibilities:
(1) Develop, recommend and encourage the adoption of a regional plan for recycling and reduction of solid wastes;
(2) Assist members in implementing any plan adopted by the Commission;
(3) Recommend management priorities to include enforcement mechanisms that will best further and enhance the goals and objectives of the Commission;
(4) Institute educational and awareness programs to further solidify public support for the Commission and its objectives;
(4) Monitor regional performance from data collected and examined under Keep America Beautiful Inc.'s Regional System guidelines and make an annual report to each member government;
(6) Seek local, State and Federal funds through membership fees, grants and loans as necessary to properly and sufficiently fund the Commission and its programs.
(Code 2003, ch. 23, art. VIII, § III; Res. of 3-7-1995)

429:
The public health, safety, economy, good order, appearance, convenience, morals and general welfare require the harmonious, orderly and progressive development of land within the County. In furtherance of the general intent of this chapter, the regulation of land development is authorized for the following purposes, among others:
(1) To encourage economically sound and stable land development;
(2) To prevent the overcrowding of land and promote desirable living conditions;
(3) To ensure the timely provision of required streets, utilities and other facilities and serves to land developments;
